<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TTimeTriggeredCallback</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TTimeTriggeredCallback" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TTimeTriggeredCallback</tt> sends callback request every <tt>Interval</tt> seconds.
Upon each callback request, the <tt>OnCallback</tt> event is raised.
The timer can be started by calling <tt>startTimer()</tt> and stopped using
<tt>stopTimer()</tt>. The timer can be automatically started when
<tt>StartTimerOnLoad</tt> is true.
</p>


<h2>Properties and methods</h2>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Member</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>Interval</tt></td><td>The gap between requests, in seconds. Defaults to 1.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>StartTimerOnLoad</tt></td><td>Whether the timer starts as soon as the page loads. Defaults to false, so a page must start it deliberately.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>startTimer()</tt>, <tt>stopTimer()</tt></td><td>Start and stop the timer from the server, including from inside a callback handler.</td></tr>
</table>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TTimeTriggeredCallback ID="Poll"
    Interval="5"
    StartTimerOnLoad="true"
    OnCallback="poll" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
A handler stops the timer once the work it was waiting for is done, so the page does not
keep polling forever.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function poll($sender, $param)
{
    if ($this-&gt;jobFinished()) {
        $sender-&gt;stopTimer();
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
A short interval means a request per client per interval, so choose it against the number
of visitors the page will have rather than against how fresh the data could be.
</p>

